The register keeps a stack of unpaid prices, scans push a price onto the stack, a void pops it if the stack is not empty, and a pay records the sum of the stack at that moment and clears it. Any items scanned after the last pay must be discarded, not counted toward any total.

The naive loop would track a single list: on scan it appends the price, on void it pops a price back to there, and on pay it appends the stack sum to a result list before clearing the stack. That misses the discard rule, because scans after the last pay do get appended to an ongoing stack. With one stack in hand, the rule becomes a matter of form: a new pay should record the current balance and then drop the zero-length stack used to match the remainder of the events. Because two register bodies share the same mechanics, what works in one sequence works in the other.

