Every remaining call goes to an else clause that has nothing inside it. That empty path makes what the function returns independent of the event list and of n. The light states are never read, no flip is made, and no reset clears anything. For example, a two-light system with a toggle of light 0 turned back on by a toggle_all would be [False, False] rather than [True, True]. The output comes straight from the asserts, not from following the instructions.

Start with n lights that are all off, then apply each event in order: ("toggle", i) flips light i, ("toggle_all",) flips every light, and ("reset",) turns every light off. The final states, True for on and False for off, are returned.

A closed-form approach tracks the number of times each light is touched. A "reset" zeroes out every light, so it is best handled first, and the remainder can be treated as starting from the all-off state. Each "toggle" on light i increases a count, and it is on exactly when that count is odd. A "toggle_all" turns each count odd or even, changing every light's state. Since no element is visited more than once, the straight count works.

        state = [False] * n
