The function processes a sequence of "arrive" and "serve" events, maintaining a queue at a server with a fixed capacity of people it can serve at once. Each serve event removes up to capacity people from the front, stopping when the queue runs out. After every event the queue is scanned from front to back, so this is an ordinary first-in-first-out queue.

When only the arrive event and one serve with capacity 1 are present, several get the same treatment, since arriving out of order results in leaving the later person waiting. The first two asserts differ only in capacity; in both the arrival order is 1 then 2 and one serve can depart. With capacity 1 the server takes 1 and leaves 2, while with capacity 5 it takes both, so the two asserts test exactly what the concurrency clause is about. The third assert feeds a serve event to an empty queue and expects [] back, which confirms the server does not pop from an empty queue.

